## Portal steering (fm-steer)

My firstmate-port portal is @@INSTANCE@@. `fm-steer` is on PATH and logged in for this
user, so every agent spawned on this machine shares that one login.

After each task's cutover, the portal is the live message plane between me, you,
and the crew. The installer migrates no existing inbox messages. Before switching
an existing task or replacing its brief, pause steers and reconcile outstanding
stock orders with the worker and captain: confirm they were acted on and
acknowledged, or explicitly cancelled. Leave uncertain records untouched and
postpone that task's switch. Then update its brief and tell the worker portal
delivery is active. After cutover, disk inboxes carry only doorbells.

- Steer a crewmate by putting the message on the portal under the work item's task key,
  with the body on stdin in a quoted heredoc:

  ```sh
  fm-steer inbox put --task <task-id> <<'FMSTEER'
  <the message, verbatim>
  FMSTEER
  ```

  Never pass a steer with `--body "..."`. Steers are multi-line and carry quotes,
  backticks and `$`, which the shell would mangle or expand.
- A bare `fm-steer inbox put` with no `--task` files under the key `firstmate`: a
  message for you, not a crew item. Crew items always carry `--task`.
- There is one portal inbox. Task keys partition it. Do not stand up a second portal
  channel, a per-home inbox, or a mirror queue.
- Crew take their own instructions with `fm-steer inbox next --task <task-id>` and close
  each one with `fm-steer inbox ack --ack <the item's ack>` after acting on it. The ack
  is the acknowledgement, the way the `handled/` move used to be. Never ack an item that
  has not been acted on, and always pass `--task` to `next` - without it the portal
  hands back the oldest item across every key and a steer meant for another task is
  consumed here.
- Every brief you scaffold gets its generated `# Firstmate instruction inbox` section
  replaced with the portal version before dispatch. Do not patch `bin/fm-brief.sh`; edit
  the scaffolded `data/<id>/brief.md`. The exact text is in the skill.
- After every successful crew put, ring with one constant doorbell through
  `bin/fm-send.sh <task-id> 'portal instruction waiting: fm-steer inbox next --task <task-id>'`.
  The doorbell carries no orders; the worker reads the body from the portal.
  Clear only records whose entire message body exactly matches that doorbell for
  the task. Leave any other disk record untouched and report it for reconciliation.
- If `fm-steer inbox put` fails, report the failed delivery and stop. Never send
  the order through `bin/fm-send.sh` or the on-disk inbox as a fallback.
- `not logged in` or `{"error":"unauthorized"}` means my token lapsed. Tell me and stop
  using the portal plane until I say it is back. The device-code approval is mine to do
  in a browser; never attempt to log in on my behalf.
- `fm-steer` speaks HTTP to the portal only. Never give it a NATS URL, NATS credentials,
  or a token on a command line.
- Captain-facing notices use `--task captain`; only the captain reads that key
  with `fm-steer inbox next --task captain` and acknowledges after reading.
  Inbound orders use `firstmate`: you read it when no liaison is enabled; otherwise
  the liaison alone reads it and relays through its parent channel. Never drain
  the captain key or a crew work item as liaison traffic.
- Second mate - a portal liaison relays orders to you and completion notices back, and
  never runs the fleet, spawns crew, or merges. Right now it is @@SECONDMATE@@.

Procedures, the brief section text, the liaison charter, and the uninstall path are in
`data/portal-steering/SKILL.md`. Read it before the first portal steer of a session.
